---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Card from "../components/Card.astro";
import Icon from "../components/Icon.astro";
import { profile, sections, experience, work } from "../data/site";
---

<Base
  title={`${profile.name} — ${profile.role}`}
  description={profile.tagline}
>
  <div class="shell">
    <header class="rail">
      <div>
        <h1 class="name">{profile.name}</h1>
        <p class="role">{profile.role}</p>
        <p class="tagline">{profile.tagline}</p>

        <nav class="nav" aria-label="Sections">
          <ul>
            {sections.map((s) => (
              <li>
                <a href={`#${s.id}`} data-section={s.id}>{s.label}</a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </nav>
      </div>

      <ul class="socials" role="list">
        {profile.socials.map((s) => (
          <li>
            <a href={s.href} aria-label={s.label} target={s.href.startsWith("http") ? "_blank" : null} rel={s.href.startsWith("http") ? "noopener noreferrer" : null}>
              <Icon name={s.icon} />
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </header>

    <main id="main">
      <section id="about" class="section" aria-label="About">
        <h2 class="section-label">About</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            I'm an engineer who likes the parts of a system where correctness is
            not obvious — the write that has to survive being retried, the token
            that has to survive being stolen, the counter two requests reach at
            the same moment.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most of my work has been at <a class="inline-link" href="https://cultcreative.asia" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cult Creative</a>,
            a creator platform where I've shipped across the backend, the admin
            dashboard, and the mobile app. Working on all three teaches you
            something narrow specialisation doesn't: how a decision in the schema
            shows up two clients later.
          </p>
          <p>
            Away from the keyboard I play ultimate frisbee — a sport with no
            referees, where players call their own fouls. It's a strange amount
            of trust to build a competitive game on, and it turns out to be good
            practice for code review.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="experience" class="section" aria-label="Experience">
        <h2 class="section-label">Experience</h2>
        <ol class="cards" role="list">
          {experience.map((e) => <Card {...e} />)}
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section id="work" class="section" aria-label="Selected work">
        <h2 class="section-label">Work</h2>
        <ol class="cards" role="list">
          {work.map((w) => <Card {...w} />)}
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section id="spirit" class="section" aria-label="Spirit of the Game">
        <h2 class="section-label">Spirit of the Game</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Ultimate calls its code of conduct <strong>Spirit of the Game</strong>:
            you can compete hard, but you're responsible for the fairness of the
            match yourself. Nobody else is going to make the call.
          </p>
          <p>
            I think that's roughly the right posture for engineering. Write the
            comment explaining why the constraint is there. Flag the race
            condition you found in someone else's PR, kindly. Say when the
            estimate was wrong. The work gets better when nobody is waiting for
            a referee.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <footer class="footer-note">
        <p>
          Built with <a class="inline-link" href="https://astro.build" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Astro</a>,
          typeset in system fonts, coloured with
          <a class="inline-link" href="https://catppuccin.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Catppuccin Mocha</a>.
        </p>
      </footer>
    </main>
  </div>
</Base>
